  "query_preview": "..High pressure centered just south of the Four\nCorners area earlier today will shift east into the southern\nPlains this weekend. Meanwhile, upstream, an upper low is\ncurrently noted over Vancouver Island and will move across the\nPacNW on Saturday. Utah will be under an increasing southwesterly\nflow aloft, while weak ripples will continue to cross the area. \nLatest analysis shows around 0.8 inches of PWAT across southern \nUtah to around 1.2 inches across northwest Utah. \n\nThe weak disturbances rippling through northern Utah tonight will\nmaintain scattered to numerous showers and isolated to scattered\nthunderstorms tonight, especially across northern Utah. This \nactivity will continue into Saturday morning before thunderstorm \nactivity increases for the afternoon hours. An unstable airmass \nwith SBCAPE values near to greater than 1kJ/kg and deep-layer \nshear of 40-50kt will bring the potential for strong to severe \nstorms. There is currently a Slight Risk for severe weather across\nnorthern Utah for Saturday, with the main threats being strong \nwinds and hail. With more widespread and stronger convection and \npotential for training storms on Saturday, the flash flood \npotential will also increase. There is currently a Marginal Risk \nof excessive rainfall across the entirety of the forecast area for\nSaturday.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will continue through Saturday night as\nthe upper low crosses Idaho. By Sunday afternoon, this low will be\nejecting east across Montana. Owing to a more stable airmass\nbehind it, convection is expected to decrease across northwest \nUtah while scattered convection will continue across the south \nand east, with continued potential for locally strong storms and \nflash flooding. While PWATs will trend lower for next week, an \nactive weather pattern will continue through at least midweek as \nseveral additional weather disturbances continue to eject across \nthe area. \n\nThis active, wet weather pattern will lead to cooler and largely\nbelow normal temperatures Saturday through as least the middle of\nnext week.",
